.TH GXWI-SECURITY-POLICY 1 "2026-10-06" "Peios"
.SH NAME
gxwi-security-policy \- see and change what this machine trusts on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-security-policy
.SH DESCRIPTION
Security Policy shows what this machine trusts: the certificate authorities
every program accepts, what this machine has added to them and what it
refuses, and the keys the kernel verifies code signatures with. It is started
from the launcher, and takes no arguments.
.PP
Its sections are down the side. Trusted lists every certificate authority in
force, shipped with Peios or added here; opening one shows who issued it,
when it is valid, its fingerprint and what it is trusted for, and offers to
export it as a PEM file, distrust it, or remove one this machine added. Add
Certificate opens a PEM or DER file and shows what is in it before anything
is trusted: one that is not a CA, has expired, or is already trusted or
distrusted is refused with the reason. Distrusted lists what is refused, to
trust again, and refuses a certificate by its fingerprint. Settings shows
how trustd is faring, with Reload, turns the certificate files under
.I /etc/ssl
on and off, and opens the permissions editor on who may change trust and who
may control trustd. Signing Keys lists the kernel's code-signing keys, which
are compiled into the kernel: nothing here changes them.
.PP
It reads from trustd, and changes the registry keys trustd reads under
.BR Machine\eSystem\eTrust ,
as the person, taking the same steps as
.BR trust (1).
As shipped, only Administrators may change what this machine trusts; anyone
else may look, and the window says once why they may not change it. Reload
is offered to those trustd's own permissions let reload it. Anything that
widens or narrows what every program accepts asks first.
.PP
The window follows trustd, so a change made with
.BR trust (1)
or
.BR reg (1)
appears within a few seconds. It needs a desktop: run with no GXWI_DISPLAY,
it says so and ends.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-security-policy.toml
Its application declaration, which lists it in the launcher.
.TP
.I /run/trustd/trust.sock
trustd's socket, where the certificates in force and its status are read.
.TP
.I /usr/share/ca-certificates/mozilla.crt
The certificates Peios ships, read to name a distrusted one.
.TP
.I /sys/kernel/security/kacs/signing_keys
The kernel's code-signing keys.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR trust (1),
.BR trustd (8),
.BR gxwi-sd-editor (1),
.BR gxwi-file-dialog (1).
.PP
Adding and distrusting certificates, and Keys and image build, at
learn.peios.org.
